WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.010
Since its founding in 1998, Rotten Tomatoes
has become the go-to site for getting a quick

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.450
idea of what film critics think of a movie.

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:11.330
Your individual tastes might vary and we all
love to watch some trashy cinema every now

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:15.480
and then but you can generally count on a
film with a high score to bring some quality

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:16.480
to the table.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.860
If you really want the cream of the crop,
though, you can look for the films that racked

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:24.330
up a unanimous critical endorsement and scored
the coveted 100%.

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:25.330
But there’s a catch.

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:29.210
The scores and the professional reviews that
generated them stay up on Rotten Tomatoes

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:32.890
forever, and almost all of them were written
when the films were first released.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:37.149
While many great movies can stand the test
of time, the years that pass can eventually

00:00:37.149 --> 00:00:38.510
reveal some flaws.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:44.679
Here are a few movies that haven’t aged
well, despite their 100% scores from the critics.

00:00:44.679 --> 00:00:46.179
The Interview

00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:49.829
If you're surprised that The Interview got
a 100 percent score, you're probably thinking

00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:54.550
of the 2014 movie about James Franco and Seth
Rogen being sent to assassinate the leader

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:55.550
of North Korea.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:56.550
“Take him out."

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:59.300
"You want us to kill the leader of North Korea?"

00:00:59.300 --> 00:00:59.980
"Yes."

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:01.140
”Whaaaaaaaat?”

00:01:02.229 --> 00:01:06.640
That movie has also aged badly, but it wasn't
exactly beloved to begin with.

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:11.480
This Interview is a 1998 Australian crime
drama starring Hugo Weaving that made a big

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:15.830
splash in indie film circles when it was released
in the United States in 2000.

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:17.680
The question is, why?

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:22.490
The film consists almost entirely of an interrogation
of Weaving's character at the hands of a police

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:23.490
detective.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:28.560
It's like an Australian Usual Suspects, but
with worse writing and no budget for flashbacks.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.360
Whether he's guilty or simply being railroaded
is kept from the audience to a degree that

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.590
makes it had to find any truth in the story
to latch onto.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:39.670
If you want proof that Hugo Weaving is a very
good actor, The Interview will accomplish

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:44.960
that, but you can find that same proof in
a much more entertaining form elsewhere.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:46.700
Roger &amp; Me

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.560
In 1989, Roger &amp; Me seemed charming.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:54.230
The work of a then-unknown documentary filmmaker
named Michael Moore, it featured the story

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:58.710
of his troubled hometown of Flint, Michigan,
and Moore's quest to confront General Motors

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:00.590
CEO Roger B. Smith.

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:05.450
The film goes into detail about the harm the
company did to Flint by laying off huge numbers

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:11.370
of workers in favor of cheaper labor in other
countries, and Moore's dogged pursuit is engaging,

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.969
even though he never manages to meet with
his subject.

00:02:13.969 --> 00:02:18.420
When it was released, this movie was nothing
like the impersonal documentaries that audiences

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:19.420
were used to.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.340
Three decades later, though, it’s a lot
harder to enjoy.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.859
Flint never recovered, and is still a city
in crisis today.

00:02:25.859 --> 00:02:30.290
Michael Moore, meanwhile, is still doing his
best to present the same "dogged everyman"

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:34.999
persona that won over audiences in this film,
but it feels far less sincere coming from

00:02:34.999 --> 00:02:38.180
an industry veteran than it did from an unknown
in his 30s.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:43.519
In 1989, Michael Moore became a liberal hero,
but these days audiences think he's overstayed

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:47.959
his welcome, regardless of their politics,
if box office receipts are any indication.

00:02:47.959 --> 00:02:52.840
In that sense, you could say that Moore's
own success has ruined his best film.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:54.689
Longtime Companion

00:02:54.689 --> 00:02:58.000
Longtime Companion was a movie that needed
to be made.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.879
Its 1990 release was groundbreaking, presenting
audiences with the first widely distributed

00:03:02.879 --> 00:03:06.790
motion picture dealing with the AIDS crisis
that had been decimating the gay community

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:07.799
for a decade.

00:03:07.799 --> 00:03:12.459
Unfortunately for modern viewers, the sadness
of its story is far from the only thing that

00:03:12.459 --> 00:03:13.849
makes it hard to watch.

00:03:13.849 --> 00:03:18.430
Craig Lucas and Norman René, the writer and
director of the movie, were both gay men who

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:23.499
lived in the world they were depicting, but
some LGBTQ activists felt like they were playing

00:03:23.499 --> 00:03:26.140
it safe in their depiction of the gay community.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:30.170
These critics called the movie out for being
afraid to depict gay men as anything other

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:34.840
than affluent and white, and to depict their
relationships as truly romantic and sexual.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:38.780
It’s clear that a major goal of the film
was to tell a story that needed to be told

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:41.239
to the people who hadn't heard (or lived)
it.

00:03:41.239 --> 00:03:45.950
In that sense, Longtime Companion was a huge
success in its time, but nobody watches it

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:48.730
anymore, and there's a reason for that.

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:49.730
The Terminator

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:54.609
Oh, Terminator - you're one of our favorite
movies ever, but that doesn't mean you've

00:03:54.609 --> 00:03:59.400
properly stood the test of time, despite how
cool your special effects were for 1984.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:04.579
We're only a decade away from Kyle Reese's
cyborg-filled future, and we still have very

00:04:04.579 --> 00:04:07.000
little of the technology that we saw.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:12.680
Unfortunately, all the hairspray, neon-colored
clothes, and Sarah Connor's roommate's music

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:17.630
throughout Terminator often leaves us cringing
as to what things were like in the '80s.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:22.530
The Terminator is justifiably looked back
on as a sci-fi-action classic, but from a

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:25.980
modern perspective, it has a few big things
working against it.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:28.190
For starters, it’s a victim of its own success.

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:32.720
It’s so influential on everything that came
after that we've seen the innovations and

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:37.000
ideas that it brought to the screen refined
dozens of times over the years.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:42.190
Even Arnold Schwarzenegger’s iconic emotionless,
unstoppable killing machine, which blew audiences

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:47.700
away in 1984 and made Arnold a household name,
seems a little less special after 30 years,

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:49.800
a handful of sequels, and a couple hundred
imitators.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:51.740
“I’ll be back…"

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:52.300
"Ha!

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:55.160
Didn't think I was going to say that, did
you?"

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:56.960
"That's what you always say!”

00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:58.740
“... I do?”

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:03.250
In that respect, it’s a lot like watching
the fight scenes in The Matrix and trying

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:08.390
to remember how innovative they were before
that style was in literally every action movie.

00:05:08.390 --> 00:05:12.500
Secondly, there's Sarah Connor, the target
that the Terminator is sent back in time to

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:13.500
execute.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:17.700
By Terminator 2: Judgment Day, we've seen
Linda Hamilton evolve into a muscular, battle-ready

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:21.400
action hero in her own right, which is the
main thing that makes revisiting the first

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:22.700
movie feel so off.

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:28.240
Here, she’s a nearly helpless, big-haired
damsel in distress who has to be rescued,

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.020
and the fact that she's being targeted solely
because she’s eventually going to be someone’s

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:36.970
mom isn’t the most empowering storyline
for a female science fiction lead.

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:38.280
“they came to fight…

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.740
For the one woman who could save their future.”

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:49.860
None of this ruins what is definitely still
a great movie, but for a film about the future,

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.970
it definitely feels dated.

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:53.500
The Searchers

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:58.520
John Ford's 1956 western The Searchers is
one of the most beautiful films ever made,

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:03.140
and features what is easily the best performance
by screen legend John Wayne.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:07.960
Wayne stars as Ethan Edwards, a Civil War
veteran who spends years looking for his niece

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:12.580
after she's kidnapped by Native Americans,
leading him to hate all Comanches.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:16.770
That hate is treated as a flaw in his character,
and that’s a far more nuanced and progressive

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:20.150
perspective than was common in westerns of
that era.

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:24.400
Unfortunately, that nuance just makes the
still-ingrained racism throughout the movie

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:25.800
all the more glaring.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:29.520
There's a whole subplot with a Native American
woman who believes herself to be married to

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:33.210
a white man who has no interest in her, played
entirely for laughs.

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:38.480
Beyond that, Ethan's blatant racism particularly
when he expresses a willingness to kill his

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:43.080
own niece if she's slept with or even been
assaulted by a Native American makes him seem

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:46.860
less like a deeply flawed anti-hero and more
like a monster.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:51.360
The Searchers is absolutely worth watching
for its cinematography and performances, but

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:55.050
there’s a lot you’ll have to ignore to
enjoy it.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:57.030
Holiday Inn

00:06:57.030 --> 00:07:02.370
Racism has to be pretty egregious to stand
out in a movie from 1942, but Holiday Inn

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:04.960
manages to pull it off in a single scene.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:09.080
If you've ever seen it, or even just watched
the trailer, you've already know exactly what

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:10.080
we’re talking about..

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.270
The premise of the Bing Crosby/Fred Astaire
musical revolves around a resort that's only

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:18.500
open on holidays, with each holiday having
its own song and dance number.

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:23.370
It famously gave the world "White Christmas,"
which would go on to become Crosby’s signature

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:27.620
song and the best-selling single of all time,
but not every holiday was so lucky.

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:31.900
Abraham Lincoln's birthday is celebrated with
a number in which the white performers are

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:36.600
dressed as black stereotypes, complete with
the exaggerated blackface makeup that was

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:39.840
common in racist 19th century minstrel shows.

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:44.880
The entire sequence has been cut from TV airings
of the film, but today, most people watch

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:47.570
it on DVD or streaming, where it's still there.

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:51.441
There's a lot to like in Holiday Inn, and
a lot of great music and dancing, but this

00:07:51.441 --> 00:07:56.510
shocking relic that was past its time even
in the ‘40s is enough to ruin the whole

00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:57.510
thing.

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:58.560
Thanks for watching!

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:01.190
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:03.430
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

